  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We start by solving $2x-5>1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & >1 \\\\ 2x & >6 \\\\ x & >3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14237546/assets/2048d360b427cfd94e92a16f7b9f8cd9ca9c88e808bf3b6205d60eebd71aa1cf.png)\n\nThen, we solve $2x-7<13:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-7 & <13 \\\\ 2x & <20 \\\\ x & <10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14237546/assets/c1a63d1c991f309dbc10a89f0416c910c2c31bde5fdf069682a6127a1f66987a.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14237546/assets/e03d04e5d2d5d571f5b80353081253087f99b2bf58fd86ae56eff3af26415b92.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is $x<10$ or $x>3.$ We can write this using interval notation as $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
